{
  "sim_type": "excel",
  "title": "Clean BSE Market Data: Text Surgery & Date Repair Pipeline",
  "scenario": "Your research desk receives a CSV export from screener.in with messy ticker symbols (spaces, exchange suffixes), company names in random case, prices stored as text that won't sum, and announcement dates in ambiguous DD/MM format. You must build a cleaning pipeline using text and date functions to produce a usable dataset for your earnings calendar and valuation model.",
  "instructions": "Build a six-station cleaning pipeline in columns D–I: TRIM the raw tickers, extract the symbol using FIND and LEFT, standardise company names to Title Case, convert text-prices to numbers, interpret dates correctly using DATEVALUE, and compute days-until-result. Work left-to-right; never touch columns A–C.",
  "learning_objective": "Apply TRIM, FIND, LEFT, PROPER, VALUE, DATEVALUE and TODAY to dismantle a real screener export, diagnose and fix text-number and date ambiguity traps, and assemble a production-ready earnings tracker.",
  "hints": [
    "TRIM-first is a reflex: invisible spaces live in A2. After TRIM, FIND locates the dot; LEFT stops one character before it.",
    "Text-prices sit left-aligned and won't SUM; VALUE() coerces them. Dates in DD/MM format must pass through DATEVALUE (or Text-to-Columns DMY) to become real serial numbers—alignment tells the truth: right = number, left = still text.",
    "For the days countdown, subtract TODAY() from the cleaned date: =cleaned_date_cell - TODAY(). For company names, PROPER forces Title Case on any uppercase mess. Flash Fill (Ctrl+E) is a one-off shortcut, not a formula—the pipeline here uses formulas because data will refresh."
  ],
